1
00:00:01,100 --> 00:00:04,023
Bottom line first: Buffett has kept Coca-Cola

2
00:00:04,023 --> 00:00:07,790
for 38 years because of the cash it hands back every year.

3
00:00:08,590 --> 00:00:12,085
All he ever paid was 1.3 billion dollars,

4
00:00:12,085 --> 00:00:13,960
between 1988 and 1994.

5
00:00:14,160 --> 00:00:17,557
This year alone the dividend is 848 million dollars.

6
00:00:17,557 --> 00:00:20,170
65% of the cost, back every single year.

7
00:00:20,970 --> 00:00:23,250
Even in the three seconds it takes to pick a can,

8
00:00:23,250 --> 00:00:24,460
about 80 dollars comes in.

9
00:00:24,460 --> 00:00:25,670
There's no reason to sell.

10
00:00:26,470 --> 00:00:27,856
Buy it today, though,

11
00:00:27,856 --> 00:00:31,157
and the yearly dividend is only 2.3% of the price.

12
00:00:31,157 --> 00:00:32,940
Same stock, so why the gap?

13
00:00:33,140 --> 00:00:36,451
Why he bought it in 1988 when it looked expensive,

14
00:00:36,451 --> 00:00:39,630
and what your dividend would be today. Let's go.

15
00:00:40,430 --> 00:00:41,403
Fun fact: at six,

16
00:00:41,403 --> 00:00:44,092
Buffett bought six bottles of Coke for 25 cents

17
00:00:44,092 --> 00:00:47,410
at his grandfather's store and sold them for 5 cents each.

18
00:00:47,610 --> 00:00:50,841
Yet it took him 52 years to buy the stock.

19
00:00:50,841 --> 00:00:53,610
What happened in the summer of 1988?

20
00:00:55,310 --> 00:00:58,230
Part one: summer, 1988.

21
00:00:59,030 --> 00:01:00,273
In his 1989 letter,

22
00:01:00,273 --> 00:01:03,150
Buffett looked back on age six: even then he

23
00:01:03,150 --> 00:01:06,420
saw the product's extraordinary pull on customers.

24
00:01:06,610 --> 00:01:08,621
A good business is one where the same customer

25
00:01:08,621 --> 00:01:09,670
comes back to buy again.

26
00:01:10,480 --> 00:01:12,400
Yet for the next 52 years,

27
00:01:12,400 --> 00:01:14,320
he only watched the stock.

28
00:01:14,510 --> 00:01:15,536
In his own words,

29
00:01:15,536 --> 00:01:19,580
he watched Coke blanket the world and still overlooked the obvious.

30
00:01:20,380 --> 00:01:23,083
Buffett wrote: only in the summer of 1988 did

31
00:01:23,083 --> 00:01:25,967
my brain finally establish contact with my eyes.

32
00:01:25,967 --> 00:01:28,190
He finally acted on what he had seen.

33
00:01:28,990 --> 00:01:31,539
That year, at 57, Buffett began buying.

34
00:01:31,539 --> 00:01:35,591
By early the next year he'd spent just over 1 billion dollars,

35
00:01:35,591 --> 00:01:37,160
for 6.3% of the company.

36
00:01:37,960 --> 00:01:41,952
By the numbers back then, it wasn't cheap.

37
00:01:41,952 --> 00:01:46,990
The stock traded at about 15 times a year's earnings.

38
00:01:47,190 --> 00:01:48,988
For every 10,000 won of stock,

39
00:01:48,988 --> 00:01:50,965
about 670 won of yearly earnings,

40
00:01:50,965 --> 00:01:52,163
while long-term U.S.

41
00:01:52,163 --> 00:01:54,680
Treasuries paid about 900 won in interest.

42
00:01:54,880 --> 00:01:56,547
He could have earned more just sitting in bonds,

43
00:01:56,547 --> 00:01:57,450
and still chose the stock.

44
00:01:58,260 --> 00:02:00,698
And the stock had already risen an average of

45
00:02:00,698 --> 00:02:02,810
18% a year for the previous five years.

46
00:02:03,010 --> 00:02:05,211
Not a sale price. Full price,

47
00:02:05,211 --> 00:02:07,260
on the day the tag went up.

48
00:02:08,060 --> 00:02:10,364
Did Buffett misread the calculator? No.

49
00:02:10,364 --> 00:02:13,790
He was looking at something other than this year's profit.

50
00:02:15,490 --> 00:02:18,360
What he saw was how Coca-Cola makes money.

51
00:02:19,160 --> 00:02:20,361
In most countries,

52
00:02:20,361 --> 00:02:23,830
Coca-Cola doesn't bottle or truck the drinks itself.

53
00:02:24,030 --> 00:02:27,328
It sells concentrate; bottling partners do the

54
00:02:27,328 --> 00:02:29,980
heavy work of filling and delivering.

55
00:02:30,780 --> 00:02:33,015
Others buy the plants and trucks; Coca-Cola

56
00:02:33,015 --> 00:02:35,250
collects for the brand and the concentrate.

57
00:02:35,450 --> 00:02:38,596
So margins are thick: last quarter it kept 33

58
00:02:38,596 --> 00:02:42,650
dollars of operating profit on every 100 dollars of sales.

59
00:02:43,450 --> 00:02:46,705
Roberto Goizueta's team, in charge since 1981,

60
00:02:46,705 --> 00:02:49,890
was spreading this business across the world.

61
00:02:50,690 --> 00:02:51,886
Ten years later,

62
00:02:51,886 --> 00:02:55,025
Coca-Cola's net income was about 3.4 times

63
00:02:55,025 --> 00:02:59,660
larger: from 1 billion dollars in 1988 to 3.5 billion in 1998.

64
00:02:59,860 --> 00:03:00,938
That's part two.

65
00:03:00,938 --> 00:03:03,094
Expensive on that year's profit,

66
00:03:03,094 --> 00:03:06,463
but Buffett was pricing the profits still to come.

67
00:03:06,463 --> 00:03:08,350
For ten years, he was right.

68
00:03:09,150 --> 00:03:11,873
So was it smooth sailing after that?

69
00:03:11,873 --> 00:03:12,780
Not exactly.

70
00:03:14,490 --> 00:03:17,050
Part two: sixteen flat years.

71
00:03:17,850 --> 00:03:19,745
After its 1998 surge,

72
00:03:19,745 --> 00:03:25,158
Coca-Cola stock only got back near that peak 16 years later,

73
00:03:25,158 --> 00:03:25,880
in 2014.

74
00:03:26,080 --> 00:03:28,810
Long enough for a newborn to reach high school.

75
00:03:29,610 --> 00:03:30,796
In his 2004 letter,

76
00:03:30,796 --> 00:03:33,480
Buffett called it a big mistake not to sell

77
00:03:33,480 --> 00:03:36,040
several large holdings during the bubble.

78
00:03:36,840 --> 00:03:38,051
So why not sell?

79
00:03:38,051 --> 00:03:42,440
Because one thing kept moving through those 16 flat years.

80
00:03:43,250 --> 00:03:46,251
The dividend: the cash a company hands its

81
00:03:46,251 --> 00:03:48,180
shareholders out of profit.

82
00:03:48,380 --> 00:03:52,810
Coca-Cola has raised it for 64 straight years since 1962.

83
00:03:52,810 --> 00:03:55,686
This year it pays 53 cents a quarter,

84
00:03:55,686 --> 00:03:57,240
2.12 dollars a year.

85
00:03:58,030 --> 00:04:01,257
Berkshire's Coca-Cola dividends grew from 75

86
00:04:01,257 --> 00:04:04,703
million dollars in 1994 to 704 million in 2022,

87
00:04:04,703 --> 00:04:07,050
and about 848 million this year.

88
00:04:07,250 --> 00:04:08,986
In his 2022 letter,

89
00:04:08,986 --> 00:04:13,190
Buffett said they rose as surely as birthdays.

90
00:04:13,390 --> 00:04:17,210
Birthdays come once a year; this company sends a gift four times.

91
00:04:18,010 --> 00:04:20,219
So what did 38 years of waiting earn?

92
00:04:20,219 --> 00:04:21,950
One multiplication tells you.

93
00:04:23,650 --> 00:04:24,780
Part three.

94
00:04:25,580 --> 00:04:30,163
By 1994 Berkshire held 100 million Coca-Cola shares.

95
00:04:30,163 --> 00:04:33,424
Two 2-for-1 splits, in 1996 and 2012,

96
00:04:33,424 --> 00:04:35,980
turned them into 400 million.

97
00:04:36,180 --> 00:04:38,785
1.3 billion dollars divided by 400 million:

98
00:04:38,785 --> 00:04:40,420
about 3.25 dollars a share.

99
00:04:41,230 --> 00:04:44,874
Each of those shares pays 2.12 dollars this year.

100
00:04:44,874 --> 00:04:47,700
400 million times 2.12 is 848 million.

101
00:04:47,900 --> 00:04:49,499
About 65% of what he paid.

102
00:04:49,499 --> 00:04:52,206
A dividend that was about 6% of cost in 1994

103
00:04:52,206 --> 00:04:55,650
now returns almost the whole stake in a year and a half.

104
00:04:55,850 --> 00:04:56,613
Same soda,

105
00:04:56,613 --> 00:04:59,820
but only the 1988 buyer gets free refills.

106
00:05:00,620 --> 00:05:02,953
But the 65% is partly an illusion.

107
00:05:02,953 --> 00:05:04,944
Sell for about $37.1 billion,

108
00:05:04,944 --> 00:05:07,277
put it into a stock yielding 2.3%,

109
00:05:07,277 --> 00:05:10,640
and the yearly dividend comes out about the same.

110
00:05:10,840 --> 00:05:12,741
So the real reasons are two.

111
00:05:12,741 --> 00:05:15,660
The gain is about $35.8 billion; 21% of it,

112
00:05:15,660 --> 00:05:16,950
about $7.5 billion,

113
00:05:16,950 --> 00:05:19,123
would go to corporate tax first,

114
00:05:19,123 --> 00:05:22,110
and what's left can't pay the same dividend.

115
00:05:22,310 --> 00:05:26,176
And a company customers return to like this is hard to find again.

116
00:05:26,176 --> 00:05:27,230
That's part three.

117
00:05:28,040 --> 00:05:30,069
One uncomfortable question remains.

118
00:05:30,069 --> 00:05:32,330
Would buying today tell the same story?

119
00:05:32,530 --> 00:05:33,206
Conveniently,

120
00:05:33,206 --> 00:05:35,960
the third-quarter report card came out on October 27.

121
00:05:37,650 --> 00:05:39,430
Let's look at the October 27 release.

122
00:05:40,230 --> 00:05:44,838
Third-quarter revenue rose 7% to 13.4 billion dollars.

123
00:05:44,838 --> 00:05:49,190
Comparable earnings per share rose 11% to 91 cents.

124
00:05:49,390 --> 00:05:50,837
Unit case volume grew 5%,

125
00:05:50,837 --> 00:05:53,500
while price and mix rose just 1%: it sold more

126
00:05:53,500 --> 00:05:54,947
without leaning on price.

127
00:05:54,947 --> 00:05:57,610
The company also raised its full-year outlook.

128
00:05:58,410 --> 00:06:02,316
Bottom line: buy today and there's no 65%.

129
00:06:02,316 --> 00:06:03,247
At $92.82,

130
00:06:03,247 --> 00:06:07,060
the yearly dividend is 2.3% of the price.

131
00:06:07,260 --> 00:06:08,788
On 100 million won of stock,

132
00:06:08,788 --> 00:06:11,189
that's about about 228만 원 a year before tax,

133
00:06:11,189 --> 00:06:13,590
and U.S. dividends arrive with 15% withheld.

134
00:06:14,400 --> 00:06:18,403
Here's the twist: today's numbers rhyme with 1988.

135
00:06:18,403 --> 00:06:22,407
The stock trades at about 30.5x a year's earnings,

136
00:06:22,407 --> 00:06:25,930
while the 10-year Treasury yields around 5%.

137
00:06:26,120 --> 00:06:28,932
On paper, bonds pay more per year again.

138
00:06:28,932 --> 00:06:33,430
We're standing at the same fridge Buffett stood at 38 years ago.

139
00:06:33,640 --> 00:06:37,989
So the one question to ask: can profits grow as

140
00:06:37,989 --> 00:06:40,580
fast as they did after 1988?

141
00:06:41,370 --> 00:06:44,223
Your yield on cost is this year's dividend

142
00:06:44,223 --> 00:06:46,260
divided by the price you paid.

143
00:06:46,470 --> 00:06:48,564
If the dividend grows 5% a year,

144
00:06:48,564 --> 00:06:51,770
in ten years it would be about 3.7% of your cost.

145
00:06:51,970 --> 00:06:53,275
On 100 million won,

146
00:06:53,275 --> 00:06:56,434
that's about about 371만 원 a year in ten years,

147
00:06:56,434 --> 00:06:57,190
before tax.

148
00:06:57,390 --> 00:07:01,670
Buffett's 65% is that same math run for 38 years.

149
00:07:02,460 --> 00:07:04,443
Of course this math could miss.

150
00:07:04,443 --> 00:07:08,730
Sugar taxes and the spread of obesity drugs could mean fewer sodas.

151
00:07:08,940 --> 00:07:10,851
A tax dispute with the IRS and weak

152
00:07:10,851 --> 00:07:13,580
emerging-market currencies could also cut profits.

153
00:07:14,380 --> 00:07:18,050
I jot these numbers down in a notebook every day; that's ValueNote.

154
00:07:18,250 --> 00:07:20,875
Coca-Cola's earning-power score is 86,

155
00:07:20,875 --> 00:07:22,603
but its price score is 5.

156
00:07:22,603 --> 00:07:26,610
A great company and a great price are different questions.

157
00:07:28,300 --> 00:07:29,270
Let's wrap up.

158
00:07:30,080 --> 00:07:32,177
1936: six bottles for 25 cents.

159
00:07:32,177 --> 00:07:34,816
1988: a purchase that looked expensive.

160
00:07:34,816 --> 00:07:37,860
1994: buying finished at 1.3 billion dollars.

161
00:07:38,050 --> 00:07:41,990
And 2026: 65% of the cost comes back every year.

162
00:07:42,790 --> 00:07:45,420
In his 1988 letter Buffett wrote: with

163
00:07:45,420 --> 00:07:48,950
outstanding businesses and outstanding managements,

164
00:07:48,950 --> 00:07:51,650
our favorite holding period is forever.

165
00:07:52,450 --> 00:07:55,249
So the question to keep isn't buy or sell.

166
00:07:55,249 --> 00:07:58,914
Do customers keep coming back to the companies you own,

167
00:07:58,914 --> 00:08:01,380
and do their profits grow every year?

168
00:08:01,580 --> 00:08:03,628
And by the way: while you watched this video,

169
00:08:03,628 --> 00:08:06,540
Berkshire collected about 15,000 dollars in Coca-Cola dividends.

170
00:08:07,350 --> 00:08:09,261
If you want more stories behind the numbers,

171
00:08:09,261 --> 00:08:10,261
please subscribe, like,

172
00:08:10,261 --> 00:08:11,390
and turn on notifications.

173
00:08:11,590 --> 00:08:13,998
The story of the companies Buffett picked in

174
00:08:13,998 --> 00:08:15,530
Japan is worth watching too.

175
00:08:15,730 --> 00:08:19,095
Which company you own keeps customers coming back?

176
00:08:19,095 --> 00:08:21,450
Tell us in the comments. Thank you.
